// The twenty-one letters of the Enochian (Angelical) alphabet, keyed by the
// Latin letter each is written for. Both traditions are served, as the
// tablets serve them: the Golden Dawn's (`gd`) and Dee's own (`dee`).
//
// `english`: the Latin letters each stands for, from the Golden Dawn's table
//   in Israel Regardie, *The Golden Dawn*, vol. 4 (Aries Press, Chicago,
//   1940), p.299: archive.org item IsraelRegardie-TheGoldenDawn-Vol4-1940,
//   leaf n291.
// `names.gd`: the letter's name in the same table (p.299, leaf n291).
// `names.dee`: the name given to Dee and Kelley in the action of 26 March
//   1583, British Library, Sloane MS 3188 (summary, as archived on 25
//   October 2026:
//   https://web.archive.org/web/20261025121954/https://www.esotericarchives.com/dee/sl3188.htm;
//   the letters as Dee drew them, f.104:
//   https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enochian_letters_(1583_manuscript).png).
//   Checked against Aaron Leitch, *The Angelical Language*, vol. II
//   (Llewellyn, 2010), Lexicon, s.v. each name, and Donald Laycock, *The
//   Complete Enochian Dictionary* (1978), s.v. each name. The Order's names
//   differ for B (Pa, Pe), F (Or, Orth), H (Na, Na-hath), N (Drux, Drun), T
//   (Gisg, Gisa) and V (Van, Vau).
// `sounds`: how the letter is said, as short respellings in ordinary
//   English letters, alternatives separated by commas, the usual first.
//   `gd` is the syllable the Order gives a letter said on its own. Mathers
//   (S.R.M.D.) and Westcott (S.A.) give the rule and examples in Regardie,
//   vol. 4, p.297 (leaf n289) and p.300 (n292): a consonant takes the vowel
//   of its Hebrew letter's name. A, D, M, N, P and S are Westcott's own
//   list; B, E, F, H, I, O, R, T, X and Z are read from the examples there;
//   G's hard or soft sound is Mathers'. Where those pages have no example
//   of the letter said on its own, the value is Crowley's Golden Dawn
//   reading of the Keys, *The Equinox*, vol. I no. 8 (London, 1912;
//   archive.org item crowley-equinox_202110): C "ca" and L "el" in
//   "ca-no-quoda" and "lores-el-qo" (p.104, leaf n134), G "ji" in
//   "ji-mi-calazodo" (p.113, n143), Q "quo" in "Quo-Co-Casabe" (p.111,
//   n141) and V "vau" in "vau-pa-ahe" (p.104, n134) and "u" in "ta-pu-ime"
//   (p.106, n136). p.300's one C, in "Heh-co-em-ah", is not C on its own:
//   it is joined there to the O after it in the word. The spellings follow
//   p.297's ("ah", "eh", "ee", "oh"), so Crowley's "ca" is written "kah",
//   and G's hard sound "ghee".
//   `dee` is the sound or sounds the letter stands for in Dee's spellings,
//   read as the English of his day. Dee's own notes beside the Keys, in
//   Meric Casaubon's print of his journal, *A True & Faithful Relation*
//   (London, 1659; archive.org item truefaithfulrela00deej, from Cotton MS
//   Appendix XLVI), give Z on its own as "zod" (pp.191, 193, 199), R on its
//   own as "ur" (MALPRG "as Malpurg", p.86), Q as "qu" (HOLQ "as Holquu",
//   p.86), I as y and j (IAD "as Yad", p.86; IAIDA "Jaida", p.79), G as soft
//   "dg" or hard (pp.192, 199) and C as k (pp.190, 192); `keys.json5`
//   carries those notes word by word. The rest, and the digraphs a letter
//   begins (ch, ph, sh, th, as in Dee's THIL and NOTHOA), are facts from
//   Leitch, vol. II, "General Notes on Angelical Phonology" (pp.33-38),
//   "Pronunciation Key (Fully Explained)" (pp.95-98) and the Lexicon's
//   entries for the letter names, and from
//   Laycock, pp.44-47 (C as s before e and i, X as ks), in our words.
// `tetragramId`: the geomantic figure the Golden Dawn gives the letter,
//   with the figure's ruler and sign, in Regardie, vol. 4, "Talismans",
//   p.77 (leaf n71). Sixteen letters have one; C, D, H, Q and X have none.
//   The glyphs on p.77 were read against the same book's alphabet on p.299.
//
// Gematria and tarot trumps were removed in 2026 (plan 080): they were
// Gerald Schueler's (*Enochian Magic: A Practical Manual*, Llewellyn, 1985,
// Table II), his own derivation from the Hebrew letters, and neither Dee
// nor the Golden Dawn gave the Enochian letters numbers. His signs for E,
// G, P, S and T also differ from p.77's figures. Only facts are taken from
// the copyrighted works cited; the wording is ours, under CC BY 4.0.
{
  A: {
    id: "A",
    enochian: "A",
    english: "A",
    names: { gd: "Un", dee: "Un" },
    sounds: { gd: "ah", dee: "a" },
    tetragramId: "amissio",
  },
  B: {
    id: "B",
    enochian: "B",
    english: "B",
    names: { gd: "Pe", dee: "Pa" },
    sounds: { gd: "beh", dee: "b" },
    tetragramId: "puer",
  },
  C: {
    id: "C",
    enochian: "C",
    english: "C,K",
    names: { gd: "Veh", dee: "Veh" },
    sounds: { gd: "kah", dee: "k, s, ch" },
  },
  D: {
    id: "D",
    enochian: "D",
    english: "D",
    names: { gd: "Gal", dee: "Gal" },
    sounds: { gd: "deh", dee: "d" },
  },
  E: {
    id: "E",
    enochian: "E",
    english: "E",
    names: { gd: "Graph", dee: "Graph" },
    sounds: { gd: "eh", dee: "e" },
    tetragramId: "albus",
  },
  F: {
    id: "F",
